.TH SIGNAL 2
.SH NAME
signal: fft, ifft, rfft, window, periodogram, convolve, firdesign, butterworth \- the discrete Fourier transform, windows and filters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "signal.m";
signal := load Signal Signal->PATH;
Biquad: import signal;

signal->init();
signal->fft(re, im);
signal->ifft(re, im);
(re, im) := signal->rfft(x);
p := signal->periodogram(x, Signal->HANN);
y := signal->convolve(x, h);
(h, err) := signal->firdesign(Signal->LOWPASS, 63, 0.1, 0.0, Signal->HAMMING);
y = signal->fir(x, h);
(bq, err) = signal->butterworth(Signal->LOWPASS, 4, 0.1);
y = signal->runiir(bq, x);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Signal
is the discrete Fourier transform and the things built directly on it.
.PP
A spectrum is two parallel arrays of reals, the real and the imaginary
parts, rather than an array of a complex adt: an array of
.B ref
would be an array of pointers and every loop over it would chase one.
.PP
.B Frequency
is in
.B cycles
.B per
.B sample
throughout, from 0 to 1/2, and never in hertz.
A sampled signal does not know its own sample rate \- that is the
caller's business, and belongs in the caller's axis labels \- and a
library that took hertz would have to be told the rate by every function
in it.
Nyquist is therefore always 0.5.
.PP
The forward transform carries no scale factor and the inverse divides by
.IR n ,
so the transform of a constant is
.I n
at bin 0.
This is said here because the other convention exists and nothing in a
test can tell a reader which one a library chose.
.SS The transform
.B Fft
and
.B ifft
transform in place, for
.B any
length and not only a power of two.
A power of two is done by Cooley-Tukey; any other length by Bluestein's
method, which rewrites the transform as a convolution that
.I is
a power of two, and so is exact rather than approximate.
No length is refused, which matters: a recording is however long it is,
and a library that silently padded to the next power of two would be
answering about a different signal.
.PP
.B Rfft
transforms a real signal into a new pair of arrays.
Its result has the full length
.IR n ,
whose upper half is the conjugate mirror of its lower half;
.B realbins
says how many are distinct.
.B Binfreq
gives the frequency a bin stands for.
Bins above
.IR n /2
stand for
.B negative
frequencies, which is the thing most often got wrong when labelling an
axis; at bin
.IR n /2
exactly, +0.5 and \-0.5 are the same frequency and +0.5 is reported.
.SS Windows
.BR Rect ,
.BR Hann ,
.B Hamming
and
.B Blackman
are chosen by the constants of those names.
A window is not a detail: the rectangular one smears a strong tone
across the whole spectrum and hides a weak one beside it, and that is
what the others are for.
.PP
There are two forms and the difference matters.
.B Window
is the periodic form, for use before a transform: it treats the
.I n
samples as one period of something that repeats, which is what the
transform assumes, and makes a tone sitting on a bin land on that one
bin.
.B Windowsym
is the symmetric form, for designing a filter: its first and last taps
are equal, and that symmetry is the whole reason a windowed-sinc filter
has exactly linear phase.
Designing with the periodic form costs the symmetry, and with it the
linear phase, silently.
.PP
.B Periodogram
is the one-sided power spectrum of a real signal, windowed and scaled so
that a sinusoid of amplitude
.I A
reads
.IR A * A /2
in its bin whatever the window and whatever the length.
Without that scaling a Hann window halves every height and the reader
concludes the signal got quieter.
.SS Convolution
.B Convolve
is the definition and
.B fftconvolve
is the same thing through the transform; both are here because the
second is only worth having if it agrees with the first, and
.IR signaltest (1)
says by how much.
.B Correlate
is the cross-correlation, at lags \-(\fBlen\fP \fIy\fP\-1) to
.RB ( len
.IR x \-1).
.SS Filters
.B Firdesign
designs a finite-impulse-response filter by windowing a sinc.
The ideal low pass is a sinc, which is infinitely long; cutting it off
is what makes the ripple, and multiplying the cut-off piece by a window
is what controls it.
The window is the entire design decision.
An odd number of taps is required, since only that has a whole-sample
delay and so an exactly linear phase.
.B Fir
filters, returning the same length as its input with the design's own
delay removed, so that a plot of the two can be read together.
.PP
A
.B Biquad
is one second-order section.
.B Butterworth
designs a cascade of them by the bilinear transform, with the cutoff
pre-warped so the \-3 dB point lands where it was asked for rather than
where the transform happens to put it.
A cascade rather than one high-order recursion, because a single
high-order recursion loses its poles to rounding.
.B Onepole
is the one-pole low pass, the shape an RC circuit has.
.B Runiir
runs a cascade over a signal;
.B Biquad.reset
clears what it remembers.
.PP
.B Firresponse
and
.B iirresponse
give the magnitude and phase of a filter at
.I nf
points from 0 to Nyquist, computed from its own coefficients.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/signal.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR circuit (2),
.IR ffts (2),
.IR math-elem (2),
.IR signaltest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B Firdesign,
.B butterworth
and
.B onepole
return nil and a diagnostic for a design that cannot be made: an even
number of taps, a cutoff at or above Nyquist, an upper cutoff below the
lower one, an order out of range.
.SH BUGS
Everything here is offline: a whole signal goes in and a whole signal
comes out.
There is no streaming interface, no overlap-add, and no way to filter a
signal that does not fit in memory.
.PP
.B Butterworth
is the only IIR design.
There is no Chebyshev, elliptic or Bessel, and no band-pass or
band-stop IIR \- for those, cascade a low pass and a high pass, which
is not the same filter a direct design would give.
.PP
.B Periodogram
is a single transform of the whole signal.
There is no Welch averaging, so its variance does not fall with the
length of the recording: a longer signal buys frequency resolution, not
a smoother estimate.
.PP
.B Fftconvolve
transforms the whole of both inputs, so filtering a long signal with a
short kernel costs a transform of the long one rather than a series of
short ones.
For a short kernel
.B convolve
is usually faster, which is the opposite of what the name suggests.
